WITH ins AS (INSERT INTO sais_resource_deadlines (resource_id, appeal_deadline, continued_benefits_deadline, hearing_request_deadline, reconsideration_deadline, judicial_review_deadline, source_file, source_hash8) VALUES 
('SAIS-EC-011-01', 'Not itself an appeal posture. Denials of a specific service or level of care are appealable through the Medicare appeals ladder -- see the appeals entry below.', 'Hospice care continues as long as the patient remains certified as terminally ill. Recertification periods are two 90-day periods followed by unlimited 60-day periods, each requiring a face-to-face encounter after the first two periods.', 'Request the PLAN OF CARE in writing. It must be established and reviewed by the interdisciplinary group and it states what services were promised and at what frequency -- it is the measuring stick for every complaint in this pipeline.', 'Where a needed service is refused, ask the hospice to document the refusal and the clinical reason in the record, and request an interdisciplinary group review. A refusal the hospice will not put in writing is itself significant.', 'Medicare hospice appeals follow the standard redetermination ladder. Fast-track appeals apply to terminations of covered care -- see the appeals entry.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-011-02', 'Grievance response: the hospice must investigate and respond. Where the response is inadequate, escalate to the state survey agency at any time -- there is no exhaustion requirement.', 'Where the patient is in a nursing facility or assisted living while receiving hospice, resident rights under 42 CFR 483.10 or state residential care rules apply IN ADDITION and are separately enforceable through the facility''s own regulator.', 'Complaint to the state survey agency: file at any time, free, no attorney required, may be anonymous. Allegations of immediate jeopardy draw a substantially faster on-site response.', 'Request the complete clinical record including the plan of care, all interdisciplinary group review notes, visit records, and medication administration records. HIPAA generally requires a response within 30 days.', 'Federal enforcement against a hospice is administrative. A private civil claim runs separately on the state limitations period, commonly 1-3 years, with malpractice procedural gates where a clinician is the defendant.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-011-03', 'Standard Medicare appeal ladder after the fast-track stage: redetermination generally within 120 days of the initial determination, then reconsideration within 180 days, then further levels generally within 60 days each.', 'REQUESTING THE FAST-TRACK APPEAL ON TIME IS THE CONTINUED-COVERAGE MECHANISM -- care generally continues while the QIO reviews. Requesting it late forfeits that continuation even if the underlying appeal eventually succeeds.', 'FAST-TRACK APPEAL TO THE BFCC-QIO: generally BY NOON OF THE DAY AFTER receiving the termination notice. This is measured in hours, not days. The QIO''s phone number is printed on the notice itself.', 'If the fast-track deadline is missed, a standard appeal remains available through the Medicare ladder -- it simply does not carry continued coverage during review. Do not treat a missed fast-track deadline as the end.', 'Judicial review is available after exhausting the administrative levels and generally requires the amount in controversy to meet an annually adjusted threshold. Most hospice disputes resolve at the QIO or redetermination stage.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-011-04', 'Survey complaint closure appeal: state-specific, commonly 15-30 days. Ask in writing for supervisory review and for the investigator''s narrative rather than only the closure letter.', 'If the patient remains in the hospice''s care and you fear retaliation, state that concern in writing to the survey agency. Retaliation for voicing grievances is prohibited under 42 CFR 418.52.', 'QIO quality-of-care complaint and survey agency complaint: file at any time. Immediate jeopardy allegations draw a substantially faster on-site investigation than routine complaints.', 'Request the hospice''s statement of deficiencies and plan of correction after any survey, and check Medicare Care Compare for the hospice''s published quality measures and survey history. Prior citations on the same requirement establish a pattern.', 'Federal enforcement against a hospice is administrative and is not appealable by the complainant. The public survey record it produces is free, admissible, and useful in any parallel civil matter.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-011-05', 'Not an administrative appeal posture. Civil and wrongful death limitations periods are state-specific, commonly 1-3 years, with malpractice pre-suit notice and certificate-of-merit gates where a clinician is a defendant.', 'SEND THE PRESERVATION LETTER NOW, naming the on-call log, visit records, and the electronic record AUDIT TRAIL. Call logs and telephony records are retained on much shorter schedules than clinical records and disappear first.', 'HIPAA records response: generally 30 days, extendable once by 30 days with written notice. Refusal or delay is separately enforceable through HHS Office for Civil Rights at no cost and requires no attorney.', 'If records are produced incomplete, request an index of what was withheld and the basis for each. Name the AUDIT TRAIL and the ON-CALL LOG specifically -- neither is typically produced unless demanded by name.', 'After death, the personal representative of the estate generally holds the right of access. Where no estate has been opened, state law determines who may request -- ask the records department which documentation it requires before assuming access is unavailable.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-011-06', 'Medicare claim appeals: redetermination generally within 120 DAYS of the Medicare Summary Notice showing the determination. Beneficiaries and their representatives may appeal charges for which they are held liable.', 'Bereavement services continue for AT LEAST ONE YEAR after the death at no additional charge. Request them at any point in that year -- eligibility does not depend on having asked at the time of death.', 'Report suspected fraud at any time. There is no deadline and no cost. The Senior Medicare Patrol will review statements with the family and assist with the referral.', 'Where a bill is disputed, request an itemized statement and compare it against the visit and level-of-care records. Discrepancies between billed level of care and documented services are the core hospice billing issue.', 'False Claims Act qui tam actions have their own limitations rules and require counsel. Ordinary beneficiary fraud reporting requires none and is the appropriate route for a family.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-FC-001', 'Appeal of a final custody order: state-specific, commonly 30 days from entry of the order. Temporary orders are usually not separately appealable but can be revisited by motion at any time.', 'Where an existing order is being violated, file a motion to enforce rather than self-correcting. Withholding a child in response to a violation generally harms the withholding parent''s position even where the underlying grievance is valid.', 'You must appear at every scheduled hearing. Non-appearance in family court commonly produces an order entered on the other party''s proposed terms, and undoing it is far harder than attending.', 'Motion to reconsider or amend a family court order: state-specific, commonly 10-30 days from entry. Motion to modify based on changed circumstances may be filed at any time thereafter.', 'Interstate custody questions are governed by the UCCJEA, which determines which state has jurisdiction -- generally the child''s home state for the prior 6 months. Filing in the wrong state wastes months and can produce a void order.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-FC-002', 'Protective order appeal: state-specific, commonly 30 days. A denied petition can generally be refiled on new incidents rather than appealed -- refiling is usually the faster route.', 'If a temporary order is in place, the full hearing date is the deadline that matters -- a temporary order generally expires if the petitioner does not appear, leaving no protection at all.', 'Emergency or ex parte protective order: available same-day in every state, and in most states available outside court hours through law enforcement or an on-call judge.', 'Crime victim compensation applications generally must be filed within 1-3 years of the crime, and most states require a report to law enforcement or another qualifying agency within a shorter window -- commonly 72 hours to 5 days. Ask about good-cause exceptions, which are common.', 'Immigration protections for survivors (VAWA self-petition, U visa, T visa) have no filing deadline tied to the abuse itself, but evidence degrades -- and a U visa requires a law enforcement certification that becomes harder to obtain over time.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-FC-003', 'Appeal of a substantiated finding placed on the state central registry: state-specific administrative appeal, commonly 30-60 days from the notice. A registry listing can bar employment in caregiving fields for years and is separately appealable from any court case.', 'Reunification services and visitation continue while the case is open -- attend every scheduled visit and every service appointment and document attendance. Missed visits are the single most frequently cited fact in termination proceedings.', 'Emergency removal (shelter care) hearing: commonly within 24-72 hours of removal. Attend with counsel; this hearing sets placement, visitation, and the case plan that governs everything after.', 'Administrative review of a case plan or a service determination: agency-specific, commonly 30 days. Request the complete case file in writing -- most states give parents a right of access with redactions for reporter identity.', 'Termination of parental rights appeals are expedited in most states with deadlines substantially shorter than ordinary civil appeals -- commonly 20-30 days. See the Parental Rights Termination entry.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-FC-004', 'Administrative appeal of a records denial: agency-specific, commonly 30 days from the denial. Many states also permit a public records appeal in parallel.', 'For a youth approaching the age of exit, request the complete file and identity documents BEFORE the exit date -- post-exit access is legally available in most states but practically much slower.', 'Request an administrative hearing to challenge inaccurate content: agency-specific, commonly 30 days from the response. Put the specific disputed entry and the correction sought in writing.', 'Reconsideration of a partial or redacted production: request an index of what was withheld and the legal basis for each withholding. Blanket redaction without a stated basis is challengeable.', 'State public records act appeals, where used as the fallback route, commonly permit court review within 30-90 days of the final agency denial.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-FC-005', 'Administrative appeal of a support order or enforcement action: state-specific, commonly 10-30 days from the notice. Income withholding contests are usually very short -- often 10-15 days.', 'Request a modification IMMEDIATELY on any income change. Support is generally modifiable only prospectively from the filing date, so every week of delay is permanent arrears at the old rate.', 'Hearing on a contested administrative support order or on a license suspension: request within the period stated in the notice, commonly 10-30 days. License suspension can usually be stayed by requesting the hearing on time.', 'Review and adjustment: most states must review an order on request every 3 years, or sooner on a substantial change in circumstances. Ask for the review by name -- it is a federal requirement.', 'Judicial review of an administrative support determination: state-specific, commonly 30 days. Interstate enforcement is governed by UIFSA, which determines which state may modify an order -- generally not the state where neither party nor the child now lives.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-FC-006', 'Appeal of a denied records request: state-specific, commonly 30 days from the written denial. Where the route is a court petition rather than an agency request, the denial is appealed as a court order.', 'Not applicable in the benefits sense. Where a contact preference or disclosure veto system exists, filing your own contact preference form is a separate and immediate step available to both adoptees and birth parents.', 'Petition to a court for good-cause release of a sealed adoption file: no filing deadline, but supporting medical need is the most frequently successful ground and is strongest when documented contemporaneously.', 'Request non-identifying medical and social history FIRST -- it is available in nearly every state without a court order and frequently resolves the underlying medical question that prompted the search.', 'ICWA information requests are not subject to standard sealing rules and have no analogous deadline. State registry registrations remain open indefinitely once filed.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-FC-007', 'TERMINATION APPEALS ARE EXPEDITED IN NEARLY EVERY STATE, commonly 20-30 days from the order and sometimes shorter. This is one of the shortest appeal windows in civil law. Preserve it the day the order enters, before deciding whether to pursue it.', 'Continue every service, every visit, and every appointment through the appeal. Compliance during the appeal period is relevant, and non-compliance after the order is routinely cited as confirming the termination.', 'Contest the reasonable-efforts finding at each review hearing, not only at the termination trial. A reasonable-efforts finding entered without objection at an earlier hearing is much harder to challenge later.', 'Motion for new trial or to reconsider: state-specific and short, commonly 10-15 days in expedited juvenile proceedings. It generally must be filed before or alongside the notice of appeal.', 'Some states permit reinstatement of parental rights in limited circumstances where a child has not achieved permanency after a defined period. This is narrow and state-specific but it exists -- ask whether your state has it.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-FC-008', 'Appeal of a delinquency adjudication or disposition: state-specific, commonly 30 days from the disposition order. Transfer-to-adult-court decisions have their own and frequently shorter review route.', 'Where a disposition includes services or placement, compliance is tracked and non-compliance produces escalation. Document completion of every required program -- completion records are what support a later sealing petition.', 'Detention hearing: commonly within 24-72 hours of detention. This is where release, conditions, and placement are set, and appearing with counsel materially changes the outcome.', 'Motion to modify a disposition: available in most states on changed circumstances or completion of requirements, generally at any time during the court''s continuing jurisdiction.', 'Sealing or expungement petition: eligibility typically begins after a waiting period following case closure or after reaching a specified age. This must be affirmatively filed -- it does not happen automatically in most states.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-FC-009', 'Appeal of a final family court order: state-specific, commonly 30 days from entry. Interlocutory and temporary orders generally are not separately appealable but can be revisited by motion.', 'Temporary orders for support, custody, and exclusive use of the home are available early in a case and govern until final judgment. Request them at filing rather than waiting -- the temporary arrangement often becomes the status quo the final order preserves.', 'You must appear at every scheduled hearing. Non-appearance commonly produces an order on the other party''s proposed terms. If you cannot attend, request a continuance in writing in advance.', 'Motion to reconsider or amend: state-specific, commonly 10-30 days from entry of the order. Motion to set aside a default judgment is separate and generally longer, commonly 30 days to 1 year.', 'Motions to modify based on changed circumstances may generally be filed at any time after the original order, and are the normal route for post-judgment change rather than appeal.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-FC-010', 'Appeal of a guardianship order: state-specific, commonly 30 days from the order. Verify your state''s number -- guardianship appeals are governed by probate rules in many states with distinct deadlines.', 'Requesting a hearing on less restrictive alternatives BEFORE a final order enters functions as the protective step -- it is materially easier to prevent an overly broad guardianship than to narrow one afterward.', 'Petition to modify, narrow, terminate, or restore rights: may generally be filed at any time. File with the court that issued the original order.', 'Petition to compel a formal accounting from the guardian: available to interested parties in nearly every state without first proving abuse, and it is usually the fastest practical step where mismanagement is suspected.', 'Petition to remove and replace a guardian for cause: available at any time. Separately, report suspected exploitation to Adult Protective Services in parallel -- the two tracks proceed independently.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-FC-011', 'Appeal of a paternity judgment: state-specific, commonly 30 days from entry. Administrative paternity orders have their own appeal window, commonly 10-30 days from the notice.', 'Support obligations generally begin accruing from establishment or from the filing date, not from the conclusion of the case. Delay in resolving a contested paternity matter does not suspend accrual.', 'RESCISSION OF A VOLUNTARY ACKNOWLEDGMENT: commonly 60 DAYS from signing or until the first related proceeding, whichever comes first. This is the shortest and most consequential deadline in this pathway.', 'Challenge after the rescission window: generally limited to fraud, duress, or material mistake of fact, and many states impose an additional outer limit (commonly 1-4 years). Genetic evidence alone is frequently not sufficient after the window closes.', 'Rebutting the marital presumption of paternity: state-specific and sometimes very short, in some states within two years of the child''s birth. Verify before assuming the general limitations period applies.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-FC-012', 'Administrative appeal of a substantiated central registry finding: state-specific, commonly 30-60 days from the written notice. This deadline is short, is stated in a letter that is easy to misread as informational, and is the only route to removal in most states.', 'Where employment is at risk pending the appeal, ask whether your state stays the registry listing during a timely-filed appeal -- several do, and the protection generally requires that the appeal be filed within the original window.', 'Request an administrative hearing on the finding within the period stated in the notice. Request the complete investigative file at the same time -- most states permit the subject access with redactions for the reporter''s identity.', 'Some states permit a request for reconsideration or an informal review before the formal hearing. Do not let it consume the formal appeal clock -- file both and state in writing that the appeal is preserved.', 'Judicial review of a final administrative registry decision: state-specific, commonly 30 days from the final decision. Expungement of an older listing is available in some states after a defined period -- ask whether yours has it.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-FC-013', 'Contest the reasonable-efforts finding at each hearing where it is made. A finding entered without objection is much harder to challenge on appeal, and reasonable efforts is one of the strongest appellate grounds in child welfare.', 'Prevention services under Family First are available to families at imminent risk WITHOUT the child entering care, and generally for a defined period (commonly 12 months) per eligibility episode. Request them before a removal, not after.', 'Request a case plan review or administrative review if the plan is not achievable. Federal law requires periodic review at least every 6 months and a permanency hearing at least every 12 months.', 'Request modification of the case plan in writing, identifying the specific barrier and the specific alternative sought. A documented request that goes unanswered is itself evidence on the reasonable-efforts question.', 'Judicial review follows the ordinary appeal route from dispositional and permanency orders, commonly 30 days. Reasonable-efforts findings are reviewable as part of those orders.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-FC-014', 'Appeal of a placement denial or a licensing denial: state-specific, commonly 30 days from the written notice. Denial of relative placement is separately reviewable from the underlying dependency case.', 'Request placement in writing IMMEDIATELY on learning of the removal. Placement decisions harden as the child settles, and the longer a child remains in an existing placement the more weight stability receives against a later relative request.', 'Request a hearing on the placement decision through the dependency court. Relatives have standing to be heard on placement in most states, though generally not full party status -- ask the court specifically for the right to be heard.', 'Request reconsideration of a licensing denial and ask specifically whether a kin-specific standard or a waiver of a non-safety licensing requirement is available. Federal rules require states to permit these.', 'Guardianship assistance and adoption assistance eligibility must generally be negotiated and executed BEFORE the guardianship or adoption is finalized. Agreements signed after finalization are frequently unavailable -- this is a permanent forfeiture point.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-HI-001', 'ERISA internal appeal: 180 days from the denial notice. Non-ERISA individual and marketplace plans: generally 180 days as well under ACA rules. Medicare and Medicaid follow separate and much shorter tracks -- see the entries below.', 'FOR ONGOING OR CONCURRENT CARE ALREADY BEING PROVIDED: appeal BEFORE the current authorization ends and coverage generally continues through the appeal. Appealing one day after termination usually forfeits this, and it is the most valuable timing rule in health coverage.', 'External review: generally 4 months from the final internal denial. Expedited external review is available concurrently with an expedited internal appeal where health is in jeopardy -- you do not always have to finish stage one first.', 'Plans must decide pre-service appeals within 30 days and post-service appeals within 60 days under ACA rules. If the plan misses its own deadline, you may generally proceed as though internal appeals were exhausted -- say so explicitly.', 'ERISA suit after exhausting plan appeals. Contractual and plan-document limitations periods apply and are frequently shorter than state law -- read the plan''s own limitations clause, not the statute.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-HI-002', 'Hospital internal billing dispute: policy-specific, commonly 30-90 days. Insurer denial of a billed service follows the standard 180-day ERISA/ACA internal appeal track.', 'Request that the account be placed on HOLD while the itemized review and financial assistance application are pending. Most nonprofit hospitals must suspend extraordinary collection actions while a financial assistance application is under review -- ask for this in writing.', 'No Surprises Act patient-provider dispute resolution for uninsured or self-pay patients billed substantially above a good faith estimate: generally 120 days from the bill. Independent dispute resolution between payers and providers has its own shorter deadlines.', 'Nonprofit hospital financial assistance applications must generally be accepted for at least 240 DAYS from the first post-discharge billing statement -- including for accounts already sent to collection. This retroactive window is very widely missed.', 'State surprise-billing and charity care statutes vary and several are broader than federal law. Check the state rule in addition to the federal one -- a claim outside the federal window may still be inside the state one.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-HI-003', 'Not an administrative appeal posture for the injured person. Board of Pharmacy complaints have no filing deadline, though evidence and memory degrade. Civil claims run on the state personal injury or malpractice limitations period, commonly 1-3 years.', 'Preserve the physical evidence immediately and refuse requests to return it. Request the complete prescription and dispensing record in writing the same week -- pharmacy records are retained for a defined period and are far easier to obtain before a dispute is formalized.', 'State Board of Pharmacy complaint: file at any time, no attorney required, no fee in nearly every state. The board''s investigative findings are frequently obtainable and are useful in any parallel civil matter.', 'Where a malpractice claim is contemplated, state pre-suit notice and certificate-of-merit requirements may apply exactly as in the medical malpractice pathway -- commonly a 90-day notice of intent and an expert affidavit.', 'Statutes of repose in many states cut off claims at a fixed outer date regardless of discovery. Minors and incapacitated persons are subject to different tolling rules -- verify the rule for the injured person''s status.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-HI-004', 'Not an administrative appeal posture. State product liability and personal injury limitations periods apply, commonly 1-6 years, often running from discovery of the injury and its cause.', 'Statutes of repose bar product claims after a fixed number of years from first sale in many states, regardless of when the injury manifested. This is a hard cutoff and it is checked far too late.', 'MedWatch report: file at any time. Request the device''s regulatory history -- PMA versus 510(k), recall status, and MAUDE reports for the same model -- as the first research step.', 'Multidistrict litigation and class settlement claim deadlines and opt-out windows are commonly 30-90 days from notice. Opting out preserves an individual claim frequently worth far more than a class share for a seriously injured person.', 'Where the device is implanted and the injury is progressive, discovery-rule analysis is fact-specific. Document the date symptoms began and the date a clinician first attributed them to the device -- these two dates frequently determine whether the claim is timely.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-HI-005', 'Standard internal appeal deadlines apply -- generally 180 days for ERISA plans. Frame the appeal as BOTH a medical necessity appeal AND a parity violation; they are decided on different grounds and raising only the first forfeits the second.', 'For ongoing treatment the plan is terminating, appeal before the authorization ends so coverage continues during the appeal. In behavioral health this frequently means appealing a concurrent review denial the same day it is issued.', 'Request the comparative analysis in writing at the same time you appeal. DOL and state regulators may also request it. Where the plan cannot produce an adequate analysis, say so explicitly in the external review request.', 'File a parallel complaint with DOL EBSA for employer plans and with the state Department of Insurance for state-regulated plans. Parity enforcement is regulator-driven and individual complaints are the primary detection mechanism.', 'External review generally 4 months from the final internal denial. ERISA suit after exhaustion. State parity laws in many states are broader than federal law and carry their own routes.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-HI-006', 'ERISA internal appeal generally 180 days. For a denial of an ongoing or imminent admission, request EXPEDITED review -- delay in this pathway is clinically consequential and expedited review exists precisely for it.', 'Appeal a concurrent review denial BEFORE the authorized days run out so coverage continues during the appeal. In residential treatment this frequently means appealing within hours, not days.', 'Expedited internal appeal and expedited external review can generally run concurrently where delay would seriously jeopardize health. Ask for both by name and have the treating clinician state the jeopardy in writing.', 'Request the plan''s comparative analysis and the specific medical necessity criteria applied. Where criteria depart from generally accepted standards such as ASAM, identify the departure specifically -- several states now require use of generally accepted criteria by statute.', 'External review generally 4 months from final internal denial. State parity and SUD-specific coverage mandates vary widely and are frequently broader than federal law.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-HI-007', 'Not an administrative appeal posture. Civil claims run on the state personal injury or malpractice limitations period, commonly 1-3 years, and pre-suit notice or certificate-of-merit requirements may apply where the defendant is a clinician or institution.', 'Withdrawal from a study is available at any time without penalty. Request in writing that data already collected be handled per the consent terms and that the withdrawal be documented in the study record.', 'IRB complaint: no deadline, and the IRB must review it. Request the IRB-approved protocol and the version of the consent form you signed -- protocol amendments made after enrollment are a common and material finding.', 'OHRP and FDA complaints: no filing deadline. Both maintain public determination letters, and prior findings against the same institution or investigator are relevant and searchable.', 'Where the sponsor is a manufacturer and the harm is attributable to the investigational product, product liability limitations and repose periods apply as in the medical device and product pathways.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-HI-008', 'Not an administrative appeal posture. State medical malpractice limitations periods apply, commonly 1-3 years from injury or discovery, and pre-suit notice and certificate-of-merit gates apply as in any malpractice claim.', 'Request the complete record and the platform session metadata in writing immediately. Platform logs are subject to retention schedules that are frequently shorter than medical record retention and are the most perishable evidence in this pathway.', 'Pre-suit notice of intent, where the state requires it: commonly 90 days before filing. Determine WHICH state''s requirements apply based on the patient''s location at the time of the encounter.', 'State medical board complaint: no deadline, no fee, no attorney required. File in both the patient''s state and the clinician''s state where they differ -- a cross-border licensure issue is independently actionable by both boards.', 'Certificate or affidavit of merit, where required: commonly due with the complaint or within 60-90 days. The qualifying expert must generally match the defendant''s specialty.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-HI-009', 'Not an administrative appeal posture. State malpractice limitations periods apply, commonly 1-3 years from the injury or its discovery, with statutes of repose in many states.', 'Preserve any physical evidence -- extracted teeth, failed restorations, prostheses -- and request that the practice preserve the original radiographs in diagnostic format. Request the record in writing before transferring care, since transfers sometimes precede record alterations.', 'Pre-suit notice of intent, where required: commonly 90 days before filing suit, and it generally tolls the limitations period while running. State Board of Dental Examiners complaint: file at any time, no fee, no attorney.', 'Certificate or affidavit of merit, where required: commonly due with the complaint or within 60-90 days after filing. The reviewing expert generally must be a dentist in the same or a comparable practice area.', 'HIPAA right of access: the practice must generally respond within 30 days and may charge only a reasonable cost-based fee. Refusal or delay is separately enforceable through HHS Office for Civil Rights and does not require any malpractice claim.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18')
ON CONFLICT (resource_id) DO NOTHING RETURNING resource_id) SELECT count(*) AS inserted FROM ins